B correctly creates non-div voting class (A) vs dividend voting class (B) and non-voting C, but assigns them backwards (Tyler gets no-div A, Alex gets div B). C assigns correctly (Alex A no-div, Tyler B with div), sole directors equal, non-voting C authorized (though C has zero economic rights). A routes Alex through spousal corp (complicates salary-only), E adds Tyler’s sons as directors (breaks equality), D lacks any non-voting class. 

Disagreements center on whether Class C’s lack of economics disqualifies C, whether B’s wording typo (“receive voting rights”) is harmless, and whether “salary-only” strictly bars any dividend rights on Alex’s shares. No consensus yet; C appears strongest match on assignment of rights + control. Need to verify Ontario share-class rules on economic rights for investors in next round.}

