PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round 0 split (B/C vs A/D) persists into round 1 but shifts heavily toward C (~2/3 of colleagues). Core constraints unchanged: equal 50/50 voting shares + sole directorships for founders; Alex needs non-dividend voting shares (salary-only); Tyler needs dividend-eligible voting shares; non-voting class pre-authorized for passive investors with no operational say.

C alone matches all four simultaneously: Alex gets non-dividend Class A voting shares, Tyler gets dividend-eligible Class B voting shares, sole equal directors, Class C non-voting authorized. B inverts dividend assignment (Tyler gets non-dividend Class A). D lacks any non-voting class and relies on board discretion for payouts (question implies share-class differentiation needed). A routes Alex via spousal corp (complicates salary-only). E adds extra directors (Tyler’s sons). 

Key disagreements: D supporters argue identical classes + director control suffice for salary vs dividends and investor limits (no need for extra classes); C supporters note this violates explicit differentiation requirement and D offers no investor mechanism. C’s Class C has zero economic rights (imperfect for investors but satisfies “no say”); B’s “receive voting rights” typo noted but irrelevant given wrong assignment. No consensus on whether Ontario law strictly requires differentiated classes or if board discretion is enough. C remains strongest match; unresolved investor-economics and payout-flexibility questions persist.}

